<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-general-kim-jong-il"><span class="enc-name">Song of General Kim Jong Il</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The &quot;Song of General Kim Jong Il&quot; is a North Korean marching song composed by Sol Myong-sun (1936-2012) to words by Sin Un-ho (1941-2020) in 1997.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-release"><span class="enc-name">Song of Release</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Song of Release is a Hurrian poem preserved in a bilingual Hurrian–Hittite version on tablets from Hattusa, dating from the fifteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/song-of-songs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Song of Songs</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Song of Songs, also called the Song of Solomon, is a book of the Hebrew Bible consisting of love poems in the voices of a woman and her lover.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Song of the Albigensian Crusade</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Song of the Albigensian Crusade is an Occitan epic poem of nearly 10,000 lines describing the crusade against the Cathars from 1208 to 1219.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea"><span class="enc-name">Song of the Sea</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Song of the Sea, also known by its opening words as Az Yashir Moshe and by a later line as Mi Chamocha, is a poem in the Book of Exodus (15:1–18).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen"><span class="enc-name">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">&quot;The Song of the Volga Boatmen&quot; is a traditional Russian song of the burlaks, the barge-haulers of the Volga River, who dragged boats upstream from the bank.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-yue-boatman"><span class="enc-name">Song of the Yue Boatman</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Song of the Yue Boatman is a short song in an ancient language of southern China, from the Yangtze River valley, said to have been recorded around 528 BC.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/songadh"><span class="enc-name">Songadh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Songadh is a town in Tapi district, south-eastern Gujarat, India, overlooked by a hill fort.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/songea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/songea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Songea</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Songea is a town in southern Tanzania and the capital of Ruvuma Region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/songhai-conquest-of-timbuktu"><span class="enc-name">Songhai conquest of Timbuktu</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · 1468</span><span class="enc-snip">The Songhai conquest of Timbuktu took place in 1468, when Sunni Ali took the city from the Tuareg, who had held it since Mali's decline.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/songhai-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Songhai Empire</span><span class="